## 4. Defensive Programming Practices

| Practice | Reason | How to implement |
|----------|--------|------------------|
| **Use strict mode** (`"use strict";`) | Prevents accidental globals and many silent errors. | Add at the top of every file (or enable via `node --use-strict`). |
| **Avoid `eval`/`Function` constructor** | They permit code injection. | Do not use them; prefer safe libraries. |
| **Never expose internal error messages** | Attackers can gain insight into your stack. | Centralized error handling (see above). |
| **Set `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`** (handled by Helmet) | Stops MIME‑type sniffing attacks. | `helmet.noSniff()` (included with `helmet()`). |
| **Enforce HTTPS** (if behind a proxy) | Prevents data leakage. | Use `helmet.hsts()` and proper proxy settings (`app.set('trust proxy', 1)`). |
| **Sanitize any data that will be reflected** (e.g., error messages) | Stops reflected XSS. | Use a library like `xss-clean` or manual escaping. |
| **Limit the size of request headers** | Prevent header‑based DoS. | `app.use(express.urlencoded({ limit: '2kb', extended: true }))`. |
| **Turn off `x-powered-by` header** | Hides Express version info. | `app.disable('x-powered-by');` (also done by Helmet). |

